In this work, we propose \longmethod(\method), a novel and unified watermarking technique against model extraction attacks on NLP models. By injecting watermarks into the prediction output of the victim model, the model owner can detect the watermark if the adversary distills the protected model.  We prove the theoretical guarantee of \method and show remarkable empirical results on text classification and sequence labeling tasks.

2306.11879.Abstract:
Recent advancements in open-domain text generation, driven by the power of large pre-trained language models (LLMs), have demonstrated remarkable performance. However, assessing these models' generation quality remains a challenge. 
In this paper, we introduce a novel method for evaluating open-domain text generation called Contrastive Distribution Methods (CDM). Leveraging the connection between increasing model parameters and enhanced LLM performance, CDM creates a mapping from the \textit{contrast} of two probabilistic distributions -- one known to be superior to the other -- to quality measures. We investigate CDM for open-domain text generation evaluation under two paradigms: 1) \emph{Generative} CDM, which harnesses the contrast of two language models' distributions to generate synthetic examples for training discriminator-based metrics; 2) \emph{Discriminative} CDM, which directly uses distribution disparities between two language models for evaluation. Our experiments on coherence evaluation for multi-turn dialogue and commonsense evaluation for controllable generation demonstrate CDM's superior correlate with human judgment than existing automatic evaluation metrics, highlighting the strong performance and generalizability of our approach. 
\footnote{Code: \url{https://github.com/PlusLabNLP/CDM}}
% }

  % to the performance measure. %thus is capable of easily scaling up to larger model/longer context
  % \violet{this gives the audience the impression that the major benefit you want to claim for CDM is scaling up to longer context, but I'm not sure whether this is indeed what you want to emphasize.}. We demonstrate how CDM can be used for evaluation by performing inference under two different paradigms: 1) \emph{Generative} CDM, which manipulates existing positive samples to create in-domain negative samples for training learnable metrics; 2) \emph{Discriminative} CDM, which directly uses the distribution discrepancy between two language models to construct the decision boundary for evaluation purposes.  Experiments on multi-turn dialogue evaluation and factuality evaluation for abstractive summarization show that, CDM achieves higher correlation with human judgement than existing automatic evaluation baselines for both application scenarios. %successfully generalizes from the partial order in the series of models and manages to correlate better with human intuition.

