\subsection{Long-Text Benchmark Evaluation}
%This part of the experiment is mainly used to validate and analyze the model's ability to handle long-text question answering. We also investigate the issue of the model struggling to correctly follow instructions when both the instructions and text are compressed. To address this problem, we propose an instruction reconstruction method. This approach allows the model to effectively answer questions even without manually inputting instructions into the encoder.


\subsubsection{Evaluation Dataset} 
LongBench \cite{c28} is a benchmark designed to evaluate the capabilities of large language models in understanding long contexts. To assess our model's performance on texts of different lengths, we selected the LongBench-E set for evaluation because it evenly covers test samples of various length ranges, allowing us to analyze the impact of length variation on performance. Due to limitations in the fine-tuning dataset, our work focuses on using the single-document QA and multi-document QA tasks for evaluation. These two document QA tasks consist of four subtasks, with each task containing between 150 to 300 samples. Detailed information on the evaluation dataset can be found in Appendix \ref{ap4}.

